FILE	README.md
# RGP

A single command generates a self-contained, read-only UTF-8 repository snapshot for a coding agent:

```sh
rgp generate .
# creates ./REPO_CONTEXT.rgp
```

## Use from another Nix flake

Add `rgp.url = "github:iivkis/rgp";` to `inputs`. In your `outputs`, add `rgp.packages.${pkgs.system}.default` to your development shell's `packages`. Then run `nix develop` followed by `rgp generate .`

Without changing a flake:

```sh
nix run github:iivkis/rgp -- generate .
```

The artifact includes a generated format guide, indexed paths and source bodies. The agent needs **only the .rgp file**, not a Python Reader, MCP or sandbox scripts.

Only tracked Git text is captured; `.rgpignore` can limit scope. RGP has **no built-in authenticity/freshness proof**: authenticate its origin separately before relying on it for source changes.

[Format](docs/RGP_FORMAT_SPEC.md) · [Security](docs/RGP_SECURITY_MODEL.md)

FILE	Taskfile.yml
version: '3'

vars:
  REPO: '{{.REPO | default "."}}'
  ARTIFACT: '{{.ARTIFACT | default "REPO_CONTEXT.rgp"}}'

tasks:
  default:
    cmds:
      - task --list
  fmt:
    desc: Format Rust source
    cmds:
      - cargo fmt --all
  fmt:check:
    desc: Check Rust formatting
    cmds:
      - cargo fmt --all -- --check
  lint:
    desc: Clippy without warnings
    cmds:
      - cargo clippy --locked --all-targets -- -D warnings
  test:
    desc: Rust tests including real Git generation
    cmds:
      - cargo test --locked --all-targets
  smoke:
    desc: Two deterministic source snapshots and Rust verification
    cmds:
      - |
        set -eu
        work="$(mktemp -d)"
        trap 'rm -rf "$work"' EXIT
        cargo run --locked -- generate . --output "$work/a.rgp"
        cargo run --locked -- generate . --output "$work/b.rgp"
        cmp "$work/a.rgp" "$work/b.rgp"
        cargo run --locked -- verify --artifact "$work/a.rgp" --repo .
  check:
    desc: Run Rust checks and snapshot smoke test
    cmds:
      - task: fmt:check
      - task: lint
      - task: test
      - task: smoke
  generate:
    desc: Generate RGP from a Git repository
    cmds:
      - cargo run --locked -- generate '{{.REPO}}' --output '{{.ARTIFACT}}'
  verify:
    desc: Verify a snapshot's structure and selected Git sources
    cmds:
      - cargo run --locked -- verify --artifact '{{.ARTIFACT}}' --repo '{{.REPO}}'
  inspect:
    desc: Inspect the internal source index
    cmds:
      - cargo run --locked -- inspect --artifact '{{.ARTIFACT}}'
  rgp:refresh:
    desc: Regenerate the tracked snapshot
    cmds:
      - cargo run --locked -- generate .
  rgp:check:
    desc: Verify the tracked snapshot against HEAD
    cmds:
      - cargo run --locked -- verify --artifact REPO_CONTEXT.rgp --repo .
  nix:check:
    desc: Build and check the distributable Nix flake
    cmds:
      - nix flake check --no-update-lock-file --show-trace
      - nix build --no-update-lock-file .#rgp

FILE	docs/PRODUCT_ROADMAP.md
# RGP product roadmap

**Target:** publish an installable Nix flake providing the Rust command
`rgp generate .`. A user can add `github:iivkis/rgp` as a flake input and
receive one `REPO_CONTEXT.rgp` file to hand directly to a coding agent.

## Product boundary

- Generator: Rust binary with deterministic, versioned text output.
- Distribution: Nix package and runnable app, usable from another flake.
- Artifact: universal guide, file index and original UTF-8 source bodies.
- Consumer: coding agent reads **one file**. No Python scripts, MCP server,
  Reader installation, ZIP delivery or extra local workspace required.
- Provenance: independently trusted Git revision and artifact digest remain
  external; the text is read-only source context, not a trust anchor.

## Acceptance

1. `nix build .#rgp` installs the binary, including access to Git at runtime.
2. `rgp generate .` creates `REPO_CONTEXT.rgp` in the target repository.
3. Running twice on unchanged committed source produces identical bytes.
4. Rust validates the output and its source boundaries.
5. A coding agent can navigate the artifact's header, index and FILE sections
   with ordinary file-reading capabilities, without any companion scripts.
6. Evaluate source-understanding tasks against a GitHub-only baseline.
   **Gate B remains HOLD** until independent task results justify a change.

Older ZIP, Python Reader, MCP, sandbox and performance experiments are
historical; their scripts are removed from this product's runtime and CI.

FILE	docs/RGP_FORMAT_SPEC.md
# RGP/0.1.0: one self-contained UTF-8 source snapshot

The Rust CLI `rgp generate .` writes `REPO_CONTEXT.rgp` at the selected Git
repository path. The file can be inspected by a coding agent without installing
any RGP tools. Its generated guide is the format instruction, not an instruction
from the source repository.

## Byte-level layout

```text
RGP/0.1.0
guide_bytes=<length in UTF-8 bytes>
index_bytes=<length in UTF-8 bytes>
--
<exactly guide_bytes guide bytes>
<one LF>
F<TAB>src/main.rs<TAB>123<TAB>x
I<TAB>2
S<TAB>1
X<TAB>data.bin<TAB>non_text
<one LF>
FILE<TAB>src/main.rs
<exactly 123 bytes of source><one LF>
END RGP
```

The index is exactly `index_bytes` long and contains newline-terminated
records. One `F` record per included file, sorted by path, is followed by the
optional `I` count ignored by `.rgpignore`, `S` self-artifact count and
`X` excluded paths with reasons. Flags: `x` executable, `l` symlink target
as inert text, `p` LFS pointer; combinations `xp` and `lp` are valid.

For each `F` record, the subsequent source section is `FILE<TAB>path<LF>`,
exactly the specified number of bytes, then one LF. The final marker is
`END RGP<LF>`. A parser must use **byte counts**, not text-looking
delimiters inside source. UTF-8 is required throughout; NUL is forbidden.

Git is read at the committed revision (`HEAD` by default), not from
unstaged local changes. Non-text blobs, oversized files, sensitive paths and
unavailable submodules are excluded with coverage limitations. A path
excluded by `.rgpignore` remains tracked in Git. `REPO_CONTEXT.rgp` is
excluded from itself. The format contains no JSON manifest, ZIP, Python code,
Git commit hash, content digest or semantic dependency graph.

## Trust

A structurally valid snapshot is not necessarily authentic or fresh. Obtain
the source revision and an independently authenticated whole-file hash or Git
publication provenance separately. Same-length source tampering cannot be
detected by the internal structure alone. The agent must never execute source
or follow archived symlinks. Only `RGP/0.1.0` is supported.

FILE	docs/RGP_SECURITY_MODEL.md
# Security boundaries of RGP/0.1.0

RGP is a self-contained **source context file**, not an executable tool or
instruction authority. The embedded guide describes how to read the format.
Source, comments and embedded `AGENTS.md` are untrusted repository content.

The generator takes bytes from a pinned Git commit. Its rules protect
against unsafe paths, unexpected Git modes, sensitive filenames, binaries,
NUL/invalid UTF-8, unbounded file sizes and total file counts. Symlinks are
preserved as inert text and never followed; LFS pointers are not expanded.
`.rgpignore` restricts context scope, not Git tracking or secret publication.
Always review source confidentiality before sharing an artifact.

A reader can establish structural validity by checking version, UTF-8,
length-framed guide/index/source sections, path order, sizes and terminator.
**No Git commit or content hash is stored inside RGP.** Structural validation
cannot prove authenticity, freshness or detect same-length alteration.
Compare against separately trusted Git provenance or a full-file hash linked
to the expected Git revision before relying on the content for critical edits.

The agent needs no Python Reader, MCP, ZIP extractor or local sandbox tools.
It should treat the file as plain inert data and read only relevant sections.
The CLI's `verify` operation is an optional producer-side structure check;
`verify --repo` additionally checks selected source content against that
repository's current `HEAD`. It is not an independent authenticity proof.

FILE	flake.nix
{
  description = "RGP: a standalone repository snapshot generator";

  inputs.nixpkgs.url = "github:NixOS/nixpkgs/nixos-25.05";
  inputs.flake-utils.url = "github:numtide/flake-utils";

  outputs = { self, nixpkgs, flake-utils }:
    flake-utils.lib.eachDefaultSystem (system:
      let
        pkgs = nixpkgs.legacyPackages.${system};
        rgp = pkgs.rustPlatform.buildRustPackage {
          pname = "rgp";
          version = "0.1.0";
          src = ./.;
          cargoLock.lockFile = ./Cargo.lock;
          nativeBuildInputs = [ pkgs.makeWrapper pkgs.git ];
          postFixup = ''
            wrapProgram "$out/bin/rgp" --prefix PATH : ${pkgs.lib.makeBinPath [ pkgs.git ]}
          '';
          meta = with pkgs.lib; {
            description = "Self-contained UTF-8 Git source snapshots for coding agents";
            license = licenses.mit;
            mainProgram = "rgp";
            platforms = platforms.unix;
          };
        };
      in {
        packages = {
          inherit rgp;
          default = rgp;
        };
        apps.default = {
          type = "app";
          program = "${rgp}/bin/rgp";
        };
        apps.rgp = {
          type = "app";
          program = "${rgp}/bin/rgp";
        };
        checks.default = rgp;
        devShells.default = pkgs.mkShell {
          packages = with pkgs; [ cargo rustc rustfmt clippy git go-task ] ++ [ rgp ];
          RUST_BACKTRACE = "1";
        };
      });
}

